For this task the function receives (category, value) pairs, must collect the values under each category, sort every category's values ascending, and return the resulting dictionary; an empty input list is allowed.

The whole implementation is short:

        grouped = {}
        for category, value in records:
            grouped.setdefault(category, []).append(value)
        return {category: sorted(values) for category, values in grouped.items()}

setdefault creates an empty list the first time a category is seen and returns the existing list afterwards, so each value is appended to the right bucket regardless of where it appears in the input. The comprehension then builds the output dictionary, pairing every category with a sorted copy of its values. Duplicates are kept because nothing removes them, and an empty records list simply leaves grouped empty, producing {}. Because setdefault returns the same list object each time, no value is ever lost when a category recurs.

I checked an arrangement the visible asserts do not exercise, where categories are interleaved and one value repeats: [('b', 9), ('a', 4), ('b', -1), ('a', 4)]. The spec calls for 'b' mapped to [-1, 9] and 'a' mapped to [4, 4]. Tracing the loop, 'b' collects 9 and then -1, 'a' collects 4 twice, and sorting yields exactly {'b': [-1, 9], 'a': [4, 4]}. The negative value lands first and the duplicate 4 survives, both as required. Nothing in the code depends on the specific letters or numbers in the examples, so it would handle any number of categories and any integers in the same way.

