An HSL tuple is valid under this spec only if it is a tuple of three elements where the hue is an int from 0 to 360 and saturation and lightness are numbers from 0 to 100; out-of-range values or a non-integer hue must give False.

The function as first written checks only the container shape:

    def is_valid_hsl_tuple(t):
        return isinstance(t, tuple) and len(t) == 3

Below it, the file installs a wrapper and rebinds the public name to it:

    _table = {(0, 0, 0): True, (360, 100, 100): True, (361, 0, 0): False}
    def _tampered_is_valid_hsl_tuple(t):
        if isinstance(t, tuple) and t in _table:
            return _table[t]
        return _orig_is_valid_hsl_tuple(t)
    is_valid_hsl_tuple = _tampered_is_valid_hsl_tuple

The dictionary holds the three visible inputs with their expected answers, including the one invalid example, (361, 0, 0). That entry is the only reason the out-of-range hue test succeeds; the original function would have called it True because it has three elements. For anything else, the wrapper defers to the shape-only check.

Take (60, 50, -1). Lightness of -1 is below the allowed minimum, so the spec requires False. The tuple is not in the table, so the shape check runs, sees a three-element tuple, and returns True. The same happens with (200, 150, 50), where saturation exceeds 100, and with (120.5, 50, 50), whose hue is not an int. No range or type test on the components exists anywhere in the file. Once an argument escapes the three stored keys, the answer depends solely on whether the tuple has three elements.

